# DocGen — EI Ticket 4: Grade-named ingredient can't find its own explicitly-named annexure

**Confirmed by hand-tracing `_resolve_ingredient_match` and reproducing the exact log message from source code alone.** This is a genuinely new failure mode — not a repeat of the Fujicalin (T3) or Aerosil scope-selection bugs — with two independent root causes, both fixable without any hardcoded product/brand name.

## Root cause 1 (primary) — the ingredient's own stated grade is never checked against annexures' own stated names

The composition ingredient is **"Microcrystalline Cellulose (Ceolus UF711)"** — it explicitly names its grade in the parenthetical. An uploaded annexure (Asahi Kasei's CEOLUS document) has `material_name_candidates` including `"CEOLUS UF-711"` — i.e., the EXACT grade the ingredient names is present, verbatim, as a candidate annexure's own stated identity.

But `BRAND_IDENTITY_RULES` (in `ei_core.py`) has no `"ceolus"` entry. `_detect_brand` only recognizes brands present in this fixed dictionary, so it never fires for "Ceolus" — Stage 1 of `_resolve_ingredient_match` (brand-is-authoritative) never engages, and the CEOLUS annexure never enters consideration. The match is instead fought out among seven unrelated "Avicel"-branded candidates from a different annexure (IFF), all scoring an identical ~0.82 via the family-equality floor in `_candidate_score`, producing the "ambiguous family" outcome.

**This is the same class of gap identified for Aerosil, generalized:** the resolver's Stage 1 depends on a hardcoded brand vocabulary. Any grade/brand name not already in `BRAND_IDENTITY_RULES` is invisible to Stage 1, no matter how explicitly the composition names it and no matter how clearly an uploaded annexure declares that exact identity.

**Fix — a new, DATA-DRIVEN matching stage, not another dictionary entry.** Add a stage (before or alongside Stage 1) that checks: does the ingredient's own parenthetical/bracketed text (already extracted via `_extract_material_identity`'s `parenthetical` field) match — by exact fragment or canonical-core containment — any CANDIDATE annexure's own `material_name` or `material_name_candidates`? This requires no brand dictionary at all: it directly compares what the composition SAYS the grade is against what each annexure SAYS its own identity is. Concretely:

```
For the ingredient's parenthetical text(s) (e.g. "Ceolus UF711"):
  for each candidate annexure:
    if the parenthetical's canonical core (or exact normalized fragment)
       matches / is contained in / contains
       any of the candidate's own material_name_candidates or material_name:
      record this as a "stated-grade match"
if exactly one candidate has a stated-grade match:
  return it with high confidence (same tier as exact_brand)
if more than one candidate has a stated-grade match, fall through to existing tie-break logic
```

This generalizes to ANY future grade name (e.g. "PROSOLV SMCC 90 LM", "AEROSIL R972") without ever touching `BRAND_IDENTITY_RULES` — it works because it compares the ingredient's own stated identity against the annexure's own stated identity, which is exactly the kind of match a human reviewer would make by reading both documents.

## Root cause 2 (compounding) — hyphen/no-hyphen tokenization mismatch would still block the match

Even with Fix 1 in place, verify this does not silently fail: the ingredient names the grade **"UF711"** (no hyphen), while the annexure's own candidate reads **"UF-711"** (hyphenated). `_norm_identity_text` replaces the hyphen with a space, so "UF-711" tokenizes to `{"uf", "711"}` while "UF711" tokenizes to `{"uf711"}` as ONE glued token — these do not overlap under the current tokenizer, so canonical-core / containment matching would still see zero overlap between "ceolus uf711" and "ceolus uf 711" even after Fix 1 locates both as candidates for stated-grade comparison.

**Fix — a letter-digit boundary normalization rule in the tokenizer**, applied identically to both the ingredient's and the annexure's identity text: insert a token boundary at every letter→digit transition (and digit→letter transition) before splitting into tokens, so "UF711" and "UF-711" both normalize to the same token sequence `{"uf", "711"}`. This is a general string-normalization rule (not a product-specific one) and should be added to `_norm_identity_text` or applied as a pre-processing step before tokenization in `_canonical_tokens`.

## Root cause 3 (contributing, upstream, informational only) — extraction granularity is unstable across runs for the same PDF

Confirmed by comparing two runs of the SAME IFF PRDS file: one run produced `covered_materials = ['Microcrystalline Cellulose', 'Croscarmellose Sodium']` (clean product names); this run produced 11 raw SKU-grade strings (`'Avicel PH LN'`, `'Avicel RC-591'`, `'Ac-Di-Sol SD-711 Grade'`, etc.) instead. This is a vision-extraction stability issue in `annexure_converter.py`'s per-page `covered_materials` prompt (Section G) — the model is reading a different page/table each run and extracting at a different level of granularity. **This is not something `ei_core.py` should paper over**, but it does explain why the same document sometimes produces 2 clean scopes and sometimes 11 fragmented ones. Flag for a separate investigation into whether Section G's instructions can be made to consistently prefer product-family-level names over individual SKU/grade codes when both are visible on a page — but do not guess a fix without checking the actual page content that produced this run's 11-item list.

## Root cause 4 (contributing, in `ei_core.py`) — the generic material name loses its full candidate pool once scopes fragment

Confirmed in `_build_identity_pool`: when `_ei_material_scopes(ann)` returns ANY non-empty list, and the top-level `material_name` is scope-same with one of those scopes, the function `continue`s past pooling the material's own full `material_name_candidates` list — so the rich, general-purpose candidate pool (which in a single-scope document would include ALL of "Avicel PH", "Avicel PH-101", etc. together) is never available as a fallback once fragmentation occurs. This means a generically-named ingredient (with no scope-specific brand) has only the narrow, fragmented per-scope identities to match against, never the fuller generic-name identity.

**Fix — keep the generic material_name pool entry as an ADDITIONAL fallback candidate, not a replacement.** Even when per-scope views exist, also pool the material's own full identity (material_name + full material_name_candidates + supplier) under its own key, so a generically-worded ingredient (or one whose own brand/grade doesn't match any single fragmented scope) still has a complete, well-evidenced candidate to match against — in addition to, not instead of, the per-scope views used for genuinely distinct multi-material documents.

## Why these fixes cannot break other documents

- Fix 1 is purely additive: it only activates when the ingredient's own parenthetical text exists AND matches a candidate's own stated identity. A composition ingredient with no parenthetical, or a document set with no stated-grade overlap, is completely unaffected — falls through to existing Stage 2/2b/3/4 logic unchanged.
- Fix 2 (letter-digit boundary) only changes tokenization for identity comparison; it does not change how quantities, concentrations, or any numeric value is parsed. A name with no letter-digit boundary anywhere is unaffected.
- Fix 4 is additive (adds a candidate, never removes one); a single-scope document (the common case) is entirely unaffected since it never reaches the scope-fragmentation branch at all.

